## 2026-09-20 — FOM comparator clarification

### Coordinator: identify the algorithms behind the combined speed table

The user asked which full-order solvers the reported ratios use. Checked the generated combined inventory against the lane designs and heat implementation: rectangular/cubic Poisson uses the direct discrete-sine inverse; heat uses discrete-sine exact semidiscrete exponential propagation; reflective waves use discrete-sine modal propagation. Burgers uses tolerance-terminated backward-Euler Newton–BiCGStab with a transform Helmholtz preconditioner, with settings selected from the same-job error/cost ladder. NS2D uses finite-difference vorticity, Arakawa advection and implicit-midpoint Newton–BiCGStab with an FFT Helmholtz preconditioner; NS3D uses dealiased Fourier velocity and CNAB2 stepping. L-shaped Poisson uses CPU sparse LU or GPU CG, with complete-query timing. The comparator settings can differ between correction rungs. The recent direct-transform baselines differ from the older explicitly iterative-CG comparisons and their speedups must remain separately labelled. No experiment, numerical change or retraction in this clarification.


